{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the people were as murmurers, etc.</b> We do not find explicitly in Scripture what this murmuring (אֲנִינוּת) was, and the verse did not come to obscure but to explain. But since we find two explicit complaints in Scripture — one, ״זָכַרְנוּ אֶת הַדָּגָה״ etc., the second, ״בּוֹכֶה לְמִשְׁפְּחוֹתָיו״ concerning matters of forbidden relations (עֲרָיוֹת) etc. — if so, surely the entire passage speaks of one matter, and all of it was a single complaint, and its source is from the pericope of ״וַיְהִי בִּנְסֹעַ״, and it is the secret of the inverted נו״ן at the end of the pericope and in the word ״כְּמִתְאֹנְנִים״. For it is wondrous in the eyes of many — and in my eyes too it is wondrous — what our Rabbis of blessed memory (Shabbat 115b) darshened, ״וַיִּשְׁמַע מֹשֶׁה אֶת הָעָם בּוֹכֶה לְמִשְׁפְּחוֹתָיו״ — concerning forbidden relations that were prohibited to them: what relevance does this complaint have here, and why did they not weep at the time when the forbidden relations were prohibited to them? And if at that time they kept silent and each one reasoned and accepted, now what had come over them that they came to recall former things and to protest about former matters that they had already fulfilled and accepted? And furthermore, did they not themselves say ״זָכַרְנוּ אֶת הַדָּגָה״, and say ״מִי יַאֲכִלֵנוּ בָשָׂר״? And without doubt all these verses, from ״וַיָּשֻׁבוּ וַיִּבְכּוּ גַּם בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ until ״וַיִּשְׁמַע מֹשֶׁה אֶת הָעָם בּוֹכֶה״, all speak of one weeping. And for this reason our Rabbis of blessed memory strove to explain the complaint by a far-removed path, for in tractate Yoma (75a) Rav and Shmuel disagree about it: one said actual fish, and one said forbidden relations and employed a euphemism, as you would say (Proverbs 30:20) ״אָכְלָה וּמָחֲתָה פִיהָ״. And according to his view, it is straightforward to say that also ״מִי יַאֲכִלֵנוּ בָשָׂר״ employs a euphemism. But behold, even according to his words we need to give good reason and understanding: what relevance does this complaint have here, and why did they keep silent when the forbidden relations were prohibited to them?" "To be sure, according to what we explained above — that the entire pericope of ״וַיְהִי בִּנְסֹעַ״ was written only to spur Israel on regarding the commandment of procreation, that they should multiply abundantly like those fish — the entire matter of the passage will come rightly, and the inverted נו״ן at the end of the pericope and the inverted נו״ן in the word ״כְּמִתְאֹנְנִים״ will tell of it, for נוּן means fish, and fish are the most unrestrained in procreation. And behold, at first, when the forbidden relations were prohibited to them, they were not distressed by this, for they thought there was no curb upon their desire — until they heard the pericope of ״וַיְהִי בִּנְסֹעַ״, which explains to them that whoever does not engage in procreation causes the Shechinah to depart from Israel. Then resentment entered their heart and they said: If only we could be fruitful and multiply like those fish with all the women, we would be assured of never coming to the departure of the Shechinah forever; but since the forbidden relations have been prohibited to us, we are the opposite of the fish — and that is the inverted נו״ן." "And this is the meaning of the passage: after they heard ״שׁוּבָה ה׳ רִבְבוֹת אַלְפֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, which spurred them on regarding the commandment of procreation to be like those fish, immediately they pondered in their heart the prohibition of forbidden relations, for this is the inverted נו״ן. Therefore ״וַיְהִי הָעָם״ — the wicked — ״כְּמִתְאֹנְנִים״. It does not say מִתְאֹנְנִים but ״כְּמִתְאֹנְנִים״, with the כ of similitude, to say: just as this אוֹנֵן — referring to one forbidden in conjugal relations — is prohibited, so has conjugal relations with all women not been permitted to us. And since the murmuring (אֲנִינוּת) was only in the heart — for they had not yet put forth their complaint from the lip outward — therefore Moses did not yet perceive their complaint. However, HaShem heard and attended to the voice of their complaint; therefore it says ״רַע בְּאָזְנֵי ה׳ וַתִּבְעַר בָּם אֵשׁ ה׳״, for through their boiling they corrupted, and the fire of desire and the fever of forbidden relations burned within them; therefore measure for measure ״וַתִּבְעַר בָּם אֵשׁ ה׳ וַתֹּאכַל בִּקְצֵה הַמַּחֲנֶה״ — either בִּקְצִינִים (the extreme ones) or בְּמוּקְצִים (the outcasts), and this matter will be explained presently."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:48.660476106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}